<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.DarkActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
    </style>

    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:background">@null</item>
    </style>


    <!--
      This extends Theme.SplashScreen, which doesn't exist as a native
      platform theme before API 31 - it resolves here via androidx.core's
      core-splashscreen compat library, which backports the same
      windowSplashScreen* system down to API 21.

      That's the actual bug this whole style had: android:background is
      a plain window-background attribute, but the compat library's
      Theme.SplashScreen implementation never reads it - it only reads
      its own custom windowSplashScreenBackground / windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon
      attrs (same names as the real platform API on 31+, just without
      the android: prefix since they're backported, not native). Setting
      android:background here was a no-op on every version this file
      applies to (API 21-30) - it silently fell through to the same
      "tiny default icon on a plain background" fallback as the
      unfixed API 31+ case, just via a different mechanism. This is why
      the fix in values-v31/styles.xml never took effect on an Android 11
      device: that override doesn't apply below API 31, and THIS file -
      the one that actually applies - was never correctly fixed until now.

      windowSplashScreenBrandingImage has no compat equivalent (confirmed
      by an earlier "attr not found" build failure when it was attempted
      without the android: prefix - the compat library genuinely doesn't
      define it), and its underlying asset (the "[icon] OS" lockup) used
      dark maroon text that only reads on a light surface anyway. Since
      splashBackground below now switches between light/dark per system
      theme (values/colors.xml + values-night/colors.xml), a single icon
      that stays legible either way was the simpler, more robust choice
      than a wordmark image that only worked in one mode. Applies to
      every API level this way - no separate values-v31 override needed
      anymore, which also removes the two-file-must-stay-in-sync risk
      that caused this exact bug in the first place (the v31 fix landed
      correctly, the base file was forgotten).

      windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon: uses drawable-nodpi/splash_icon.png
      (a plain flat copy of public/icons/icon-512x512.png), NOT
      @mipmap/ic_launcher. Pointing this attribute at the mipmap
      reference made the OS treat it as an adaptive icon (it resolves
      through mipmap-anydpi-v26/ic_launcher.xml, which is background +
      foreground layers) and apply its own adaptive-icon mask + upscale
      on top of an image that was already a flattened, rounded square -
      producing the oversized, blurry, forced-circular result reported.
      A plain drawable reference outside the mipmap/adaptive-icon system
      is displayed as-is, at its own native resolution, no extra
      masking.
    -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/splashBackground</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/splash_icon</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/AppTheme.NoActionBar</item>
    </style>
</resources>